From 3525d2dfd66ec501cd008f815c5e7988e170da6a Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Sat, 3 Oct 2026 02:20:39 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] media: qcom: iris: treat a completely full HFI command queue
 as full

iris_hfi_queue_write() accepted a packet when it exactly fitted into the
free space. The shared queue uses read_idx == write_idx to mean "empty",
so a packet that fills the queue to the last byte moves write_idx onto
read_idx. The driver reports success and raises the interrupt, but the
firmware sees an empty queue and never processes the packet. The next
write then sees a fully empty queue and starts overwriting the packets
that were never consumed.

Reject the write unless there is strictly more free space than the
packet needs, which is what the Venus HFI queue code does.

A user-space replay of the function (scaled to an 8 KiB queue with
1 KiB packets) shows the 8th packet being accepted with
write_idx == read_idx before this change, and -ENOSPC after it.

Co-Authored-By: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01XHbRyvZ53xawh6pBdmacMe
---
 drivers/media/platform/qcom/iris/iris_hfi_queue.c | 9 ++++++++-
 1 file changed, 8 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/drivers/media/platform/qcom/iris/iris_hfi_queue.c b/drivers/media/platform/qcom/iris/iris_hfi_queue.c
index 60354964e..ae6c2d359 100644
--- a/drivers/media/platform/qcom/iris/iris_hfi_queue.c
+++ b/drivers/media/platform/qcom/iris/iris_hfi_queue.c
@@ -21,7 +21,14 @@ static int iris_hfi_queue_write(struct iris_iface_q_info *qinfo, void *packet, u
 		empty_space = read_idx - write_idx;
 	else
 		empty_space = IFACEQ_QUEUE_SIZE - (write_idx -  read_idx);
-	if (empty_space < packet_size)
+
+	/*
+	 * read_idx == write_idx means "empty". A packet that fills the queue
+	 * completely would make the write index catch up with the read index
+	 * and the firmware would see an empty queue, so one slot is always
+	 * kept free.
+	 */
+	if (empty_space <= packet_size)
 		return -ENOSPC;
 
 	queue->tx_req =  0;
-- 
